Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The method that registers URL rules on blueprint objects (`Blueprint.add_url_rule` in `src/flask/sansio/blueprints.py`, lines 413-441) defers registration by storing an anonymous function (lambda) in the deferred setup functions list instead of immediately calling the setup state\'s registration method.\\n\\n**The Deferral Mechanism:**\\n\\nWhen `Blueprint.add_url_rule` is called, it doesn\'t directly register the URL rule with an application. Instead, it calls `self.record()` (line 433), passing a lambda function that captures the registration parameters (rule, endpoint, view_func, options). The `record()` method (lines 224-230) appends this lambda to `self.deferred_functions`, a list that stores all deferred setup operations.\\n\\n**Why Deferral is Necessary:**\\n\\nThe blueprint may not be registered with an application when `add_url_rule` is called. The setup state object (`BlueprintSetupState`) that contains the application reference, URL prefix, subdomain, and other registration-time information doesn\'t exist until the blueprint is registered via `Blueprint.register()` (line 273). The setup state is created in `make_setup_state()` (line 321) during registration.\\n\\n**When Deferred Functions Execute:**\\n\\nThe deferred functions are executed later when `Blueprint.register()` is called (lines 334-335):\\n```python\\nfor deferred in self.deferred_functions:\\n    deferred(state)\\n```\\nAt this point, the `state` parameter (a `BlueprintSetupState` instance) is available with the application reference and registration options. The lambda then calls `state.add_url_rule()` (line 434), which in turn calls `self.app.add_url_rule()` (line 110) to register the rule with the actual Flask application.\\n\\n**What Would Break with Immediate Registration:**\\n\\nIf the code tried to call `s.add_url_rule()` directly in `Blueprint.add_url_rule`, it would fail because:\\n1. The setup state object (`s`) doesn\'t exist yet - it\'s only created during blueprint registration\\n2. The application reference isn\'t available - blueprints are created independently of applications\\n3. Registration-time options (url_prefix, subdomain, name_prefix) aren\'t known until registration\\n4. The blueprint\'s name and URL prefix need to be applied to endpoints and rules, which requires the setup state created during registration\\n\\nThis deferred execution pattern allows blueprints to be defined and configured before being registered with an application, supporting Flask\'s modular application architecture where blueprints can be registered multiple times with different options.\\n</start_of_answer>"}' + '\n')
